THIRD-PARTY DATA

The MIT licence above covers Cryonav's own source code. It does NOT relicense
the datasets cached under data/, which remain under their own terms and carry
their own attribution requirements. Each data file records its licence in a
`license` field; the obligations are summarised here.

OpenStreetMap - the pedestrian network (data/streets/), urban form
(data/urban/) and Gulf cooling shelters, fetched via the Overpass API.
  (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, licensed under the Open Database Licence
  (ODbL) v1.0: https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/
  ODbL is share-alike. Attribution is rendered on every map view in the app and
  is required in any redistribution or derived database.

Meta / World Resources Institute Canopy Height Maps v6 - the 1.194 m canopy
raster (alsgedi_global_v6_float/chm), via the AWS Open Data registry.
  CC-BY-4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
  Attribution required.

USGS / NASA Landsat Collection 2 Level-2 - 30 m land surface temperature,
accessed via the Microsoft Planetary Computer. Public domain (U.S. Government
work); attribution appreciated, not required.

NASA / JPL ECOSTRESS ECO_L2T_LSTE v003 - 70 m peak-hour surface temperature,
via NASA LP DAAC. NASA open data; free to use with attribution.

Maricopa Association of Governments Heat Relief Network - the Phoenix cooling
shelters, from MAG's public ArcGIS feed. MAG's own accuracy disclaimer is
stored alongside the data and surfaced in the app.

FortyGuard Temperature API - the observed 2 m ambient field. Used under the
terms granted for the FortyGuard Global AI Hackathon '26. No FortyGuard data,
credential or derived raster is redistributed in this repository; the API key
lives only in an untracked .env, and calibration outputs are regenerated by the
consumer's own key. FortyGuard and Temperature API are trademarks of their
respective owner; Cryonav is an independent integration and is not endorsed by
or affiliated with FortyGuard.
